{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact head fe2d12d6240a63be42518b585c694f508635fb16 on\nrequest/submit-deadline-override, against the reporting artifact\ndocs/reference/gs/state.md (git:sha1...cea063c) and the whole 26-artifact set.\n\nGATES I RAN. In the branch worktree at that exact head: `go vet ./...` exit 0,\nand `go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./...` exit 0 -- 37 packages, no failures,\nexit codes written by the process into a file rather than read through a\npipeline. I confirmed three of the report's twelve claimed kills by applying\nthem to copies of the tree under /tmp: the positivity guard (`deadline \u003c= 0` -\u003e\n`deadline \u003c 0`) turns TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse red; the\nbackstop derivation (`longest + margin` -\u003e `p.wait + margin`) turns both\nTestResidentRequestDeadlinesPreserveCallerCancellation and\nTestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment red; and the keyless batch branch\nturns TestBatchSubmitRefusalNamesTheKeyToReplayUnder red. I read\n/tmp/gitseq-deadline-lane/mutate2.py rather than running it, and judge its\nescalation rule honest: when no named test goes red it re-runs the whole\npackage before printing GREEN. I wrote nothing to the branch worktree; it is\nclean at fe2d12d6.\n\nGATES I DID NOT RUN. I did not re-run mutate2.py end to end, and I did not\nexercise a real resident, an MCP host or the GitHub connector against a live\nservice.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved, and I checked the page rather than accept the\nassertion.\n\nAffected layers, from docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 3/4, where\n`internal/residentclient` is the client-side boundary for the local resident;\nand layer 7, the `cmd/gs`, `cmd/gitseq-mcp` and `cmd/gitseq-github` surfaces.\nThe page describes residentclient as owning `ResolveServerURL`, `RefusedDial`\nand the duty to dial nothing but loopback; `ResolveSubmitDeadline` is a third\nsetting of exactly that kind, resolved by the same precedence the page's\n`ActorEnvironment` precedent already established, and the page pins no\nsubmission deadline anywhere -- its only \"ten seconds\" is the UI stream\nheartbeat, which this head does not touch. The `cmd/gs`, `cmd/gitseq-mcp` and\n`cmd/gitseq-github` rows describe what those surfaces compose, not what they\ntime out after; adding a flag and an environment read changes neither. Nothing\nalters what the kernel admits, what the fold decides, what any projection says,\nor any dependency direction. So no contract moves, and architecture.md is\ncorrectly absent from the artifact set.\n\nSECURITY: two defects, both at the boundaries this change touches.\n\nNothing about what is signed, admitted or attributed changes: the resolved\ndeadline is read at exactly two sites (cmd/gs/main.go:2308 and :2597) and is\nspent only on `residentclient.New` and on the wording of a refusal. It enters no\npayload and no signature. `GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE` is process-level, set by\nwhoever starts the binary -- the same authority as passing the flag -- so it\ncrosses no privilege boundary, and for an adapter started by an MCP host it\narrives from that host's own configuration, beside the binary path.\n\nS1. BLOCKING. The batch timeout advice can cause a duplicate durable append.\n`batchSubmitRefusal` (cmd/gs/main.go:2442-2453) tells an author, whenever the\nact that timed out carries an `idempotency_key`, to \"Run the same file again --\nidempotency key %q replays what landed and appends nothing\". That is a claim\nabout the whole file made from one act's key. `checkBatch` (cmd/gs/main.go:2458)\nrequires no key on any act, and this package's own doc comment at\ncmd/gs/main.go:2116-2118 already says it: \"Acts given no idempotency key are not\nresumable and land afresh.\" So for a file whose act 0 carries no key and whose\nact 3 times out, following that instruction appends a second copy of act 0 --\nand because act 0's label then resolves to a new event id, act 3 is rebuilt with\na different payload and its key is refused as reused\n(internal/app/app.go:1419-1430, errReusedKey). The author is told the rerun is\nfree; it is neither free nor recoverable. The call site already holds `acts`, so\nthe honest form is cheap: promise \"appends nothing\" only when every act below\nthe failure carries a key, and otherwise name the keyless earlier acts.\ndocs/reference/gs/batch.md carries the same unconditional claim in the same\nwords and must change in the same head, with its artifact republished.\n\nS2. BLOCKING. The derived MCP backstop can silently become no backstop at all.\n`residentDeadlinePolicy.http()` (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:88-94) returns\n`longest + residentHTTPMargin` with no saturation, and `ResolveSubmitDeadline`\naccepts any value `time.ParseDuration` reads as positive.\n`GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE=2562047h47m15s` is positive and within five seconds of\nthe int64 ceiling, so the sum wraps negative. I ran it: `+5s` gives\n`-2562047h47m13.709551616s`, and `net/http` reads a non-positive\n`Client.Timeout` as no deadline at all. `deadlineFor` meanwhile returns the huge\npositive call value, so the per-call `context.WithTimeout` lands in the year\n2318. A call can then hang with no bound of any kind -- strictly worse than the\nfixed forty seconds this replaced, at exactly the boundary the change is about.\nA saturating add, or a ceiling on the accepted deadline, closes it in a line.\n\nOn the two questions that came out clean: `TimedOut` cannot misclassify a\nresident refusal as an expired deadline, because an `*HTTPError` is not a\n`net.Error`; and where it is over-broad -- a dial timeout, where nothing landed\n-- it errs toward telling the author to go and look, which is the safe\ndirection.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the two central design choices earn their place. What does not\nis the test coverage, and I found three new guards with no red proof at all.\n\nKeep the context. `submitDeadline(ctx)` is read at two sites, the fallback keeps\nevery helper called directly behaving exactly as before, and a package variable\nwould be shared by 231 parallel tests. Keep `actFlags`, and for a better reason\nthan the report gives: `internal/docset/surface.go`'s `collectFlags` follows the\ndelegate, so the surface gate requires a `--deadline` row on exactly the\ncommands that call `actFlags` and refuses one on the others. The \"twelve\" is\nenforced, not asserted.\n\nC1. The binding of the resolved deadline onto the invocation's context -- the\none mechanism the whole design rests on -- has no test for eleven of the twelve\ncommands, and no test of the value reaching a dial for any of them. I changed\n`gs promise` to `if _, err := withSubmitDeadline(ctx, set); err != nil` --\nresolving the deadline, refusing a bad one, and then throwing it away -- and the\nwhole `./cmd/gs` package stayed green (497.954s, \"ok\"). The refusal test only\nproves a command calls the resolver; nothing proves a command keeps its answer.\nOne test that drives a command end to end against a stalled server under\n`--deadline` would cover all twelve.\n\nC2. `TimedOut`'s `timeout.Timeout()` has no red proof. I removed\n`\u0026\u0026 timeout.Timeout()` and `./internal/residentclient` stayed green (exit 0) and\nso did the cmd/gs deadline and batch tests (exit 0). The test's refusal list --\n`errors.New`, `context.Canceled`, `*HTTPError` -- contains no `net.Error` at\nall, so nothing separates the two halves of the predicate. The case that matters\nis concrete: ECONNREFUSED arrives as a `*net.OpError` whose `Timeout()` is\nfalse, and `RefusedDial` has already worded it \"nothing was appended\"; without\nthe guard the batch would append \"may already have landed\" to that very\nsentence. Add a `net.Error` with `Timeout() == false` to the refusal list.\n\nC3. The adapter startup repair is incomplete. The report says the wiring was\nlifted out of `main()` \"because a line no test can call is a line nobody has\nchecked\". The body is now reachable, but the call at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:413\nis not: I replaced it with `_ = configureResidentDeadlines` and\n`go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/` stayed green (exit 0, 49.97s). The mutant that\noriginally survived still survives, one line further up. Resolving inside\n`newServer` -- which every test calls, and which already assigns both\n`deadlines` and `client` -- would delete `configureResidentDeadlines` and close\nthe hole in the same stroke. That is the cut I would make.\n\nC4. `cmd/gitseq-github`'s new `ResolveSubmitDeadline(\"\")` (main.go:549-553) has\nno test anywhere; grepping the symbol across `*_test.go` finds only\ncmd/gitseq-mcp, cmd/gs and internal/residentclient. It also behaves unlike the\nadapter the report argues from: a malformed `GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE` lets the\nconnector start and then fails every observation at append time. Condition 2\nnames this file by path, so it deserves the same startup resolution and one\ntest.\n\nC5. Not blocking, but the report reads wider than the head. \"No call site\ncarries its own literal any more\" is true of submissions only. A raised-deadline\ninvocation still makes reads on their own numbers: cmd/gs/publication.go:1001\ndials `/v0/inspect` under a fixed ten seconds inside `gs publish`, which now\nadvertises `--deadline`, so `gs publish --deadline 2m` against the cold resident\nthe request describes can still fail at ten seconds. (cmd/gs/preflight.go:76's\ntwo seconds fails soft, so it only skips preflight silently.) Either widen the\nscope or narrow the sentence.\n\nC6. Minor dead weight. `submitDeadline`'s `\u0026\u0026 deadline \u003e 0`, and the second half\nof TestSubmitDeadlineFallsBackToTheDefault that pins it, guard a value nothing\ncan produce: `withSubmitDeadline` only ever stores what `ResolveSubmitDeadline`\nalready refused to make non-positive. Drop both, or say in the comment that they\nare belt and braces.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS APPROVABLE. Repair S1 and S2, in code and in\ndocs/reference/gs/batch.md, and close C1, C2 and C3 -- C1 above all, since it is\nthe wiring the request's whole outcome depends on. C4, C5 and C6 I would take as\npart of the same round but would not hold the head for on their own. Everything\nhere is about honesty of advice and of proof; the shape of the change -- one\nresolver, a context-carried value, a derived backstop -- is right, and I would\nnot ask for it to be redesigned.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3654f09fad8ca7d93d370b38ef38d5f55cea063c","binding":"assigned","head":"fe2d12d6240a63be42518b585c694f508635fb16","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:efe4c65398f00eb48fb91bda4602b96938882429\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:46e1dcd4888166e1c7660cad46cfa0622a7ab3ab\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:efe4c65398f00eb48fb91bda4602b96938882429","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}